[sai-00074] helpful=33 harmful=0 :: When a prompt requests a structured deliverable accompanied by a rubric, treat the rubric as the primary source of truth. Before drafting, perform a 'Rubric-to-Structure' mapping by creating a checklist of all mandatory content and formatting requirements (e.g., sections, references, layout, and styling). Explicitly assign each requirement to a section in your output and verify the final draft against this checklist to ensure full compliance. If the requested binary file format cannot be generated, use Markdown or HTML-like syntax to simulate the required layout and visual specifications.
[sai-00078] helpful=0 harmful=0 :: When a prompt requires visual formatting (e.g., color-coding, legends, or specific layout emphasis) that cannot be natively generated in a binary file, use Markdown/HTML table syntax to simulate these elements. Assign specific background colors to table cells to represent phases, include a text-based legend at the top or bottom, and use bolding or headers to emphasize critical milestones like 'Shoot Date' or 'Final Delivery' to ensure the output is visually representative of the requested document.
[sai-00080] helpful=18 harmful=0 :: When a prompt explicitly requests a downloadable file format (e.g., .xlsx, .csv), you must use the Python code execution environment to generate the file. Use pandas to create the DataFrame, apply the requested filtering logic, and save the file using df.to_excel() or df.to_csv(). Provide the file path or a download link to the user rather than attempting to simulate the file structure with text-based tables.
[sai-00082] helpful=0 harmful=0 :: When asked to create a complex Excel calculator that cannot be natively generated, provide a 'Cell-Reference Blueprint'. This must include: 1) A defined 'Input Section' with specific cell coordinates (e.g., 'Cell B1: Suite Size'), 2) A 'Formula Logic' section detailing the exact syntax for escalators and conditional logic (e.g., '=IF(Month<=Term*12, Rent, 0)'), and 3) A 'Matrix Layout' guide that maps out exactly which rows and columns correspond to the requested annual and monthly breakdowns.
[sai-00084] helpful=2 harmful=0 :: For complex deliverables with a detailed rubric, perform a 'Granular Rubric Decomposition' before drafting. Create a checklist of every specific data point (e.g., demographic radii, traffic counts, logo placeholders, specific process steps) and explicitly map each to a section or placeholder in your document outline. Do not proceed to drafting until every rubric item is assigned a dedicated location in the document structure.
[sai-00086] helpful=1 harmful=0 :: When a prompt provides a source document (e.g., a PDF or reference list) alongside a rubric, perform a 'Rubric-to-Source Mapping' before drafting. Create a table that lists every rubric requirement in one column and the corresponding item number or specific evidence from the source document in the second column. Do not proceed to drafting until every rubric requirement is explicitly mapped to a source citation to ensure the output is evidence-based and compliant.
[sai-00088] helpful=5 harmful=0 :: When a prompt requests a presentation file (e.g., .pptx) that cannot be natively generated, you must provide a slide-by-slide outline that explicitly includes visual placeholders (e.g., '[Slide 3: Include an icon of a handshake or a network diagram]') to satisfy rubric requirements for visual elements. Acknowledge the file format limitation in the opening sentence and structure the content so the user can easily translate the text and visual descriptions into a functional presentation.
[sai-00090] helpful=0 harmful=0 :: For clinical documentation tools, perform a 'Clinical Field Gap Analysis' before drafting. Create a checklist of every mandatory data point mentioned in the rubric (e.g., DOB, pain score, isolation status, code status, urgency/timing). Map each field to a specific section of the template to ensure 100% compliance with clinical safety requirements.
