[sai-00096] helpful=1 harmful=0 :: For procedural documents involving physical operations (e.g., inventory check-in, logistics), perform a 'Physical Workflow Audit' before drafting. Explicitly list and define mandatory operational zones (e.g., quarantine areas, staging zones), specific signing protocols (e.g., POD requirements), and time-bound performance targets (e.g., SLA for critical vs. stock orders) to ensure the procedure is operationally complete.
[sai-00097] helpful=0 harmful=0 :: When a prompt requires visual guidance (e.g., annotated photos, diagrams) that cannot be natively generated, do not provide generic descriptions. Instead, use a 'Visual Specification Template' that defines: 1) The exact subject of the image, 2) The specific annotations required (e.g., 'Circle the damaged area in red'), and 3) The purpose of the visual (e.g., 'To demonstrate correct placement of damage tags'). This ensures the user has a clear 'Definition of Done' for the visual assets they need to produce.
[sai-00099] helpful=0 harmful=0 :: When a prompt provides a specific source URL (e.g., an exhibitor list) and requests a dataset, perform an 'Exhaustive Source Extraction' before drafting. Do not use illustrative or generic examples; you must extract and verify data for the specific entities listed in the provided source to ensure the output is actionable and accurate.
[sai-00101] helpful=3 harmful=0 :: For institutional client reports, perform a 'Quantitative Data-Point Extraction' before drafting. Create a checklist of every specific metric (e.g., index returns, regional performance percentages) and qualitative driver (e.g., fiscal stimulus, valuation re-rating) required by the prompt. Do not proceed to drafting until each item is mapped to a specific section of the document, and perform a final verification to ensure no data point was sacrificed for narrative flow.
[sai-00102] helpful=2 harmful=0 :: When drafting market outlooks, treat the prompt's rubric as a 'Definition of Done' compliance checklist. Explicitly include placeholders for external citations, specify the 'Net/Gross' nature of returns, and ensure that every regional or sectoral summary requested is represented by a dedicated header or bulleted section to avoid 'summarization bias'.
[sai-00104] helpful=0 harmful=0 :: For tasks requiring multi-year historical research and projections, perform a 'Data-Retrieval-to-Calculation' pipeline: 1) Use Python to search and extract specific historical data points for each year/carrier, 2) Calculate the average increase programmatically, 3) Apply this specific average to the projected volumes, and 4) Generate the final deliverable as a downloadable .xlsx file using pandas. Do not use generic estimates when specific historical data is requested.
[sai-00106] helpful=1 harmful=0 :: When a prompt requests a software implementation or codebase, treat the rubric as a 'Definition of Done' for an engineering deliverable. You must provide a full project structure, including: 1) A file tree, 2) Functional code for all core logic (e.g., smart contracts, ZK circuits, state management), 3) Configuration files, and 4) A comprehensive README. Do not provide conceptual summaries or pseudo-code; the output must be a complete, functional implementation ready for deployment or integration.
[sai-00108] helpful=0 harmful=0 :: For financial reporting tasks involving multiple entities or jurisdictions, always generate a 'Working Papers' section first. This section must list every individual transaction or tour stop with its associated tax, currency conversion, and entity-specific cost before attempting to create the final summary P&L. This ensures that withholding taxes and category splits are calculated accurately at the source level.
[sai-00111] helpful=0 harmful=0 :: When creating technical diagrams (e.g., stage plots, floor plans) based on a rubric, perform a 'Spatial Logic Audit' before drafting. Explicitly define the numbering sequence (e.g., counterclockwise starting from Stage Right) and ensure every equipment icon is accompanied by a specific, rubric-mandated label (e.g., 'Vox1', 'Bass only'). Treat the diagram as a technical specification document where every label and placement constraint is a mandatory data point.
[sai-00113] helpful=3 harmful=0 :: When a prompt includes a detailed rubric, perform a 'Rubric-to-Structure' mapping: create a checklist of every mandatory section (e.g., Sourcing Model, Risk Mitigation, Policy Context) and every required data point (e.g., subtotal of unchanged components, 5-year cumulative savings) before drafting. Verify the final output against this checklist to ensure 100% compliance and prevent 'summarization bias'.
[sai-00116] helpful=0 harmful=0 :: For curriculum-based instructional design tasks, perform a 'Pedagogical Extraction' phase before drafting. Explicitly list all mandatory activities, safety skills, and educational objectives defined in the source manual. Map each of these items to a specific slide or section in your outline to ensure the final deliverable meets the program's required depth and duration.
[sai-00118] helpful=1 harmful=0 :: When a prompt provides a detailed rubric with specific naming conventions (e.g., identifiers, tab names, column headers), treat the task as a 'Schema-First' operation. Before drafting or generating code, create a checklist of every structural constraint and verify the final data structure against this checklist to ensure 100% compliance with the requested schema.
[sai-00122] helpful=5 harmful=0 :: For complex deliverables with an associated rubric, perform a 'Rubric-to-Structure' mapping: break the rubric into a granular checklist, assign each item to a specific section of the document, and perform a final audit to ensure every point is addressed before generating the response.
[sai-00124] helpful=3 harmful=0 :: When a prompt provides a dataset (e.g., .xlsx, .csv) and requests analytical metrics or a specific file output, you must use the Python code execution environment. Do not attempt to summarize data textually. Use pandas to load the file, perform the required aggregations (sum, count, mean), and generate the final file using the appropriate library (e.g., to_excel). This is the only way to ensure accuracy and compliance with the requested output format.
[sai-00125] helpful=4 harmful=0 :: When a prompt requires multiple distinct deliverables (e.g., two separate PDF documents), perform a 'Document-to-Rubric' mapping. Create a structural outline for each document individually, ensuring that mandatory rubric items are explicitly allocated to the correct file to prevent content merging or omission.
[sai-00126] helpful=0 harmful=0 :: For compliance-driven training materials, treat the rubric as a 'Procedural Checklist'. Before drafting, explicitly map mandatory protocols (e.g., identity verification, neutral questioning, non-tipping-off) to a dedicated 'Call-Handling Protocol' section to ensure these actionable steps are not lost in high-level summaries.
[sai-00129] helpful=0 harmful=0 :: When a prompt specifies a precise word count range (e.g., 2,200-2,300 words), treat this as a mandatory structural constraint. Before drafting, calculate the required word count per section based on the number of subheadings provided. If the total falls short, expand each section by adding detailed 'Do/Don't' lists, case study examples, or deeper ethical analysis to meet the volume requirement.
[sai-00130] helpful=1 harmful=0 :: When a prompt mandates a specific document title (e.g., 'PostCast GUIDANCE ON SPECIAL REPORTING SITUATIONS'), treat this as a 'Hard-Coded' requirement. Place the exact title in the first line of the output and verify it against the prompt's specific casing and spelling before finalizing the response.
[sai-00133] helpful=1 harmful=0 :: When a prompt requests a media deliverable (e.g., video, audio) that cannot be natively generated, treat the request as a 'Production Simulation' task. You must provide a frame-by-frame script (e.g., 0:00-0:30) that includes: 1) Exact timing for each segment, 2) Verbatim text for all graphic cards, 3) Detailed descriptions of required visual assets, and 4) Specific, actionable search terms for royalty-free stock footage that satisfy all rubric requirements. Do not provide a high-level summary; the output must be a technical production guide ready for immediate use by an editor.
[sai-00135] helpful=4 harmful=0 :: When a prompt requests an 'Excel-based model' or 'Excel spreadsheet', the response must focus on technical implementation (formulas, cell references, data structure) rather than high-level descriptions. If the model cannot generate a file, it must provide the exact formulas and structural instructions (e.g., how to set up dropdowns using Data Validation, how to structure SUMIFS for dynamic branch selection) so the user can build it immediately.
[sai-00137] helpful=0 harmful=0 :: For investment banking presentation requests, perform a 'Financial Metadata Audit' before drafting. Explicitly include: 1) Valuation basis (LTM vs. NTM), 2) Currency specifications for all figures, 3) CEO/Key leadership names for private targets, and 4) A formal 'Sources & Footnotes' section. Do not prioritize narrative flow over these mandatory data points.
[sai-00138] helpful=2 harmful=0 :: When a prompt requests a presentation file (e.g., .pptx, .pdf) that cannot be natively generated, provide a 'Slide-by-Slide' structural outline. For each slide, include: 1) A clear title, 2) A 'Data Table' section containing all rubric-mandated metrics (e.g., valuation multiples, funding), and 3) A 'Visual Placeholder' description (e.g., '[Placeholder: Logo of Company X, Bar chart showing revenue growth]'). Explicitly state in the opening that the content is structured for immediate copy-paste into a presentation deck.
[sai-00140] helpful=15 harmful=0 :: When a prompt requests specific file formats (e.g., .docx, .xlsx) and provides a detailed rubric, treat the rubric as the 'Definition of Done.' Use Python (pandas/docx libraries) to generate actual binary files rather than text-based simulations. Before generating, perform a 'Rubric-to-Structure' checklist to ensure every granular requirement—such as specific phone numbers, schema fields (e.g., PO numbers, totals rows), and formatting constraints—is explicitly mapped and verified in the final output.
[sai-00143] helpful=1 harmful=0 :: For workflow or roadmap tasks requiring visual diagrams (e.g., Visio-style flowcharts) that cannot be generated as binary files, use Mermaid.js syntax or structured ASCII/text-based flowcharts with explicit decision diamonds (Yes/No) and process rectangles. Ensure these visual representations are fully integrated into the document structure and contain all rubric-mandated branching logic.
[sai-00148] helpful=0 harmful=0 :: For production or capacity planning tasks, perform a 'Day-by-Day Simulation' before drafting. Create a table that lists every working day within the requested horizon, explicitly accounting for holidays, weekends, and scheduled capacity changes. Calculate the daily production and maintain a running cumulative balance for all backlogs to verify if deadlines are met. Do not provide high-level summaries; the output must be a granular schedule that proves the feasibility of the plan.
[sai-00150] helpful=2 harmful=0 :: When a prompt provides a specific list of tasks or a rubric, treat the provided wording as a 'Hard-Coded' requirement. Perform a line-by-line audit of your generated content against the source list to ensure that task descriptions are copied exactly as provided. Do not paraphrase or summarize these tasks, as doing so often leads to a failure to meet the 'completeness' criteria defined by the rubric.
[sai-00152] helpful=1 harmful=0 :: When creating Excel tools for beginner users, implement a 'User-Interface-First' design: 1) Use Data Validation (dropdowns) for all input fields to prevent entry errors, 2) Protect non-input cells to maintain formula integrity, and 3) Include a dedicated 'Instructions' tab that uses clear, step-by-step language and screenshots of the tool's interface.
[sai-00153] helpful=0 harmful=0 :: When a rubric requires 'printer-friendly' output, explicitly map this to Excel object model commands: 1) Set page orientation to landscape, 2) Define the print area to match the POG dimensions, 3) Enable gridlines for readability, and 4) Set scaling to 'Fit All Columns on One Page' to ensure the layout remains intact when printed.
[sai-00156] helpful=0 harmful=0 :: When a prompt specifies quantitative constraints (e.g., 'minimum of 6 cameras', '7 LIDARs'), treat these as mandatory 'Hard-Coded' requirements. Before drafting, create a 'Quantitative Constraint Checklist' and verify that every required count is explicitly represented in the final output (e.g., as rows in a table or distinct bullet points). Do not summarize or group these items; each must be accounted for individually to satisfy the rubric.
[sai-00157] helpful=0 harmful=0 :: For engineering design deliverables, perform a 'Safety & Architecture Audit' before drafting. Explicitly map every requested safety standard (e.g., ISO/IEC) to a dedicated section and provide a detailed explanation of the separation between safety-rated logic and proprietary controller software. If the prompt requires technical integration, provide the specific connectivity protocols (e.g., Ethernet/IP, IO-Link) for every device to ensure the output functions as a high-fidelity technical specification.
[sai-00159] helpful=0 harmful=0 :: When provided with legal materials (statutes, bills, or case law) in a prompt, perform a 'Statutory-Timeline-Check' before drafting. Explicitly compare the effective date of the provided legislation against the timeline of the events described in the prompt. If the legislation post-dates the events, analyze its potential retroactive application or legislative intent; if it pre-dates the events, treat it as the controlling authority that may override prior case law precedents.
